Here is an example implementation of the JavaScript library you described:

```
class FormValidator {
  constructor(formSelector, fields) {
    this.form = document.querySelector(formSelector);
    this.fields = fields;
    this.errors = {};
    this.validateOnBlur();
    this.form.addEventListener('submit', this.onSubmit.bind(this));
  }

  validateField(field) {
    const input = field.querySelector('[name]');
    const name = input.getAttribute('name');
    const value = input.value.trim();
    const rules = this.fields[name].rules;
    let isValid = true;
    let errorMessage = '';

    for (let rule of rules) {
      switch (rule) {
        case 'required':
          isValid = isValid && value !== '';
          errorMessage = 'This field is required';
          break;
        case 'email':
          isValid = isValid && /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(value);
          errorMessage = 'Please enter a valid email address';
          break;
        case 'phone':
          isValid = isValid && /^[+]?[(]?[0-9]{1,4}[)]?[-\\s\\./0-9]*$/.test(value);
          errorMessage = 'Please enter a valid phone number';
          break;
      }
      if (!isValid) {
        this.errors[name] = errorMessage;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (isValid) {
      delete this.errors[name];
    }
    this.showErrors();
  }

  validateOnBlur() {
    for (let field of this.form.querySelectorAll('.form-field')) {
      const input = field.querySelector('[name]');
      input.addEventListener('blur', () => {
        this.validateField(field);
      });
    }
  }

  onSubmit() {
    for (let field of this.form.querySelectorAll('.form-field')) {
      this.validateField(field);
    }
    if (Object.keys(this.errors).length > 0) {
      this.showErrors();
      event.preventDefault();
    }
  }

  showErrors() {
    for (let name in this.fields) {
      const field = this.form.querySelector(`[name="${name}"]`).closest('.form-field');
      const error = field.querySelector('.form-error');
      if (error && this.errors[name]) {
        error.textContent = this.errors[name];
        field.classList.add('form-field--invalid');
      } else {
        if (error) error.textContent = '';
        field.classList.remove('form-field--invalid');
      }
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const validator = new FormValidator('#my-form', {
  name: { rules: ['required'] },
  email: { rules: ['required', 'email'] },
  phone: { rules: ['phone'] },
});
```

To use the library, create an instance of the `FormValidator` class with the CSS selector of your form and an object specifying the validation rules for each field. The validation rules are specified as an array of strings, with each string representing a validation rule.

In this example, we're checking that the `name` field is required, the `email` field is required and a valid email address, and the `phone` field is a valid phone number. You can add additional rules as needed (such as minimum and maximum length, alphanumeric characters only, etc.).

The library adds a `form-error` element to each field that fails validation and updates it with the appropriate error message. It also adds a `form-field--invalid` class to the field element to apply the appropriate styling.

To trigger validation on form submission, the `onSubmit` method validates each field and displays any errors. If there are any errors, it prevents the form from submitting. Validation is also triggered on field blur (`validateOnBlur` method).

Here's an implementation of a Trie data structure in PHP.

```
class TrieNode {
    public $children = [];
    public $isEndOfWord = false;
}
class Trie {
    private $root;
    function __construct() {
        $this->root = new TrieNode();
    }
    function insert(string $word) {
        $current = $this->root;
        for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($word); $i++) {
            $char = $word[$i];
            if (!array_key_exists($char, $current->children)) {
                $current->children[$char] = new TrieNode();
            }
            $current = $current->children[$char];
        }
        $current->isEndOfWord = true;
    }
    function search(string $word) {
        $current = $this->root;
        for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($word); $i++) {
            $char = $word[$i];
            if (array_key_exists($char, $current->children)) {
                $current = $current->children[$char];
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return $current->isEndOfWord;
    }
    function startsWith(string $prefix) {
        $current = $this->root;
        for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($prefix); $i++) {
            $char = $prefix[$i];
            if (array_key_exists($char, $current->children)) {
                $current = $current->children[$char];
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}

/* Example usage */
$trie = new Trie();
$trie->insert("apple");
echo $trie->search("apple");   // Output: 1
echo $trie->search("app");     // Output: 0
echo $trie->startsWith("app"); // Output: 1
$trie->insert("app");
echo $trie->search("app");     // Output: 1
```

Here's an example code for the horizontal fixed navbar with a drop-down menu using CSS:

HTML code:

```
<nav class="navbar">
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">About</a></li>
    <li class="dropdown">
      <a href="#">Services &#9662;</a>
      <ul class="dropdown-menu">
        <li><a href="#">Web Development</a></li>
        <li><a href="#">Graphic Design</a></li>
        <li><a href="#">Digital Marketing</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#">Products</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

CSS code:

```
body {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.navbar {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  background-color: #333;
  color: #fff;
  z-index: 9999;
}

.navbar ul {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

.navbar li {
  flex: 1;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 15px;
  position: relative;
}

.navbar a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: #fff;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}

.navbar a:hover {
  background-color: #555;
}

.dropdown-menu {
  display: none;
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  min-width: 200px;
  padding: 10px;
  background-color: #333;
}

.dropdown:hover .dropdown-menu {
  display: block;
}

.dropdown-menu li {
  padding: 10px;
}

.active {
  background-color: #555;
}
```

Explanation:

1. The HTML code defines a basic navigation menu with a list of links and a dropdown menu inside one of the list items.
2. The CSS code styles the navbar by making it fixed at the top of the screen, setting a dark background color and white text, and using flexbox to align the list items horizontally. It also styles the links with a hover effect and sets the dropdown menu to be hidden by default and appear when the parent list item is hovered over.
3. The "active" class can be added to any of the links to give it a different background color, indicating the current page. This can be done dynamically with JavaScript or added manually to the HTML.

Here's a possible implementation in Pascal:

program BubbleSort;

const
  MAX_SIZE = 100; { maximum size of the array }

var
  arr: array[1..MAX_SIZE] of integer;
  n, i, j, temp, numSwaps: integer;

begin
  { get size of array and validate input }
  repeat
    write('Enter size of array (1 to ', MAX_SIZE, '): ');
    readln(n);
  until (n > 0) and (n <= MAX_SIZE);

  { get elements of array }
  writeln('Enter ', n, ' integers:');
  for I := 1 to n do
    readln(arr[i]);

  { display unsorted array }
  writeln('Unsorted array:');
  for I := 1 to n do
    write(arr[i], ' ');
  writeln;

  { bubble sort algorithm }
  numSwaps := 0; { initialize swap counter }
  for I := 1 to n-1 do
    for j := 1 to n-i do
      if arr[j] > arr[j+1] then
      begin
        { swap adjacent elements }
        temp := arr[j];
        arr[j] := arr[j+1];
        arr[j+1] := temp;
        { increment swap counter }
        numSwaps := numSwaps + 1;
      end;

  { display sorted array and number of swaps }
  writeln('Sorted array:');
  for I := 1 to n do
    write(arr[i], ' ');
  writeln;
  writeln('Number of swaps: ', numSwaps);

  readln; { wait for user to press enter }
end.
